WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.617
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.617 --> 00:00:13.359
Great ideas, where great ideas are ideas
that solve a problem in a unique way,

00:00:13.359 --> 00:00:18.333
usually happen when two old ideas meet
together for the first time.

00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:21.910
So great ideas are not new ideas.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:26.430
They are usually a combination of existing
ideas.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:31.250
And that's were brainstorming is powerful
because it brings together people

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:36.440
with different experiences so they can all
merge their ideas together.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.470
So that's where design thinking become
effective because

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:42.240
it promotes that collaborative approach.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.675
Bring people from different parts of the
organization together to bring you their

00:00:46.675 --> 00:00:49.761
different knowledge, their different
expertise, and

00:00:49.761 --> 00:00:53.055
provide their input into how you're gonna
solve a problem.

00:00:53.055 --> 00:00:57.091
And by combining those ingredients
together, you can come up with new and

00:00:57.091 --> 00:00:58.991
creative ways to solve a problem.

00:00:58.991 --> 00:01:01.993
[MUSIC]

00:01:01.993 --> 00:01:05.086
One thing that we have learned over the
years is that,

00:01:05.086 --> 00:01:07.640
that process doesn't work with everyone.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:11.410
So we have developed our own variation.

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:14.570
That also kinda work well with introverts.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:19.550
So we can ask people to individually think
about 10 or

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:23.320
15 ways in which they can address the
problem.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.180
Do that ahead of time.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:28.550
And then meet as a group, share their
ideas.

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:32.143
And then engage into a more structured
brainstorm.

00:01:32.143 --> 00:01:35.530
So a combination of individual idea
generation.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:39.378
And combined group idea generation can
work really well.

00:01:39.378 --> 00:01:42.592
[MUSIC]

00:01:42.592 --> 00:01:45.697
It's all about putting yourself in your
user's shoes.

00:01:45.697 --> 00:01:48.460
Spend time with your users.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:50.020
Ask them questions.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.220
Think ahead.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:52.960
What do you want to learn?

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.800
And draft some interview questions.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:58.370
And then engage in a conversation.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:01:59.900
Don't make it an interview.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.630
Engage in a conversation, but be prepared.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:03.550
What do you want to learn?

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.280
Be a good listener.

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:13.900
And try to listen to the stories that your
user is gonna share with you.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:19.920
Ask them about the last time they have
faced a challenging situation, and

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.210
how they addressed that challenging
situation.

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:26.330
That's a way for you to learn about your
user.

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:30.810
If they are willing to let you observe
them while they go

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:35.020
through their everyday life, that's a
great way to get deeper insights.

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:41.243
If you start discovering what is a problem
that you want to solve, and

00:02:41.243 --> 00:02:47.115
they let you solve that problem for them,
or work with them to solve the problem.

00:02:47.115 --> 00:02:51.039
That's a great way to really understand
what the problem is.

00:02:51.039 --> 00:02:54.626
[MUSIC]

00:02:54.626 --> 00:02:57.778
When you observe them, you see what they
actually go through.

00:02:57.778 --> 00:03:00.130
But when you actually do it,

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:06.720
that's when you can really understand how
difficult some things may be.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.870
Or what are the emotions that some
challenges are creating.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:14.864
I may tell you that changing the tire of
my car is difficult but in order for

00:03:14.864 --> 00:03:19.469
you to understand why it's difficult, you
would probably want to do it yourself.

00:03:19.469 --> 00:03:24.414
Or, I may tell you that I find a
particular task challenging but you may be

00:03:24.414 --> 00:03:29.782
able to better appreciate how challenging
it is when you try to do it yourself.

00:03:29.782 --> 00:03:33.201
[MUSIC]

00:03:33.201 --> 00:03:36.491
You want something that you can take back
to your user and

00:03:36.491 --> 00:03:38.383
your user can interact with it.

00:03:38.383 --> 00:03:44.030
They can feel it, they can play with it,
they can use it one way or another.

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:49.030
So the thing about quick and inexpensive
ways that you can make your early idea

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:53.480
usable, so you can go back to your user,
your customer, and get feedback.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:57.650
And that feedback, now, is giving you
information.

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:00.620
Is this a good idea, or is this a bad
idea?

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:03.840
Oftentimes, from either your users'
reaction,

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.640
you may discover that the problem that you
thought they had is not there and

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.330
they have a different problem.

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:14.489
But you also learn more about what they
want, what their preferences are.

00:04:14.489 --> 00:04:17.957
So you're using this process of generating
ideas and

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:22.579
taking them back to your user and getting
feedback is a way to define your

00:04:22.579 --> 00:04:26.294
